#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152935} :activity/id #uuid "1a693dfa-5b4d-5813-b09f-df9a73d9e43a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6de9a5a5-ab7e-5d26-821c-9f50ad7ae54d" :topic/math-academy-id 2787} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153434 :tutorial/content "A quantifier is a special symbol (operation) that may transform a predicate into a statement.\n\nThe **universal quantifier** \"$\\forall$\" means \"for all.\" The formula $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ means that *every* $x$ in the universal set $U$ gives a true statement when substituted into the predicate $P.$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+,\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0$$\n\nreads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **For all** $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+,$ $\\sqrt{a} > 0$\n\nThis can be further rephrased using plain English as simply:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The square root of any positive real number is positive.*\n\nSometimes, we can omit the \"$\\in U$\" part if the domain is clear from the context and write $\\forall x, \\, P(x).$\n\nFor example, if it's clear that $n$ is an integer, then the following is a valid statement involving the universal quantifier:\n\n$$\\forall n,\\, (2 \\mid n)$$\n\nIn plain English, we might say the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number two divides any integer.*\n\nNote that this statement is *false*." :tutorial/id #uuid "6b69eeeb-3757-5954-bd3d-96c7a93010dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7991 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ba03659f-f524-5dbb-ae6c-372a02577563" :step/math-academy-id 145572 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151812 :question/id #uuid "8c1240c3-a5ac-5c44-8381-4b82c199f591" :question/math-academy-id "e-12741"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "875d7f2a-43ec-5635-8e72-48ae83d0339b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401879}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "c8132653-5a1b-5476-a04e-ce1b65c3f133" :step/math-academy-id 145573 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152937}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153447 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the formal definition of the universal quantifier in connection to predicates of one variable.\n\nThe **universal quantification** of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x).$$ This statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the *entire* universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate equals the universal set $U{:}$ $$T_P = U$$\n\nFor example, consider the quantified statement\n\n$$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0.$$\n\nNotice that the universal set is $U = \\mathbb{R}$ and the truth set of the underlying predicate\n\n$$P(a): \\:\\: \\sqrt{a} > 0$$\n\nis $T_P = (0,\\infty).$ Since $T_P$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0$ is false." :tutorial/id #uuid "13634493-c706-51c0-adcf-200f064583eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8309 :tutorial/title "The Formal Definition of the Universal Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "7f06bd0e-607f-5cfc-b053-5817229eb606" :step/math-academy-id 145576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151810 :question/id #uuid "61af13e3-ff6a-5d2e-b888-9ec31afe00a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049552}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381741}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "e2e9fee2-4bef-5bcf-9273-f2b635d59f5c" :step/math-academy-id 145577 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153446 :tutorial/content "The existential quantifier \"$\\exists$\" means \"there exists\". The formula\n\n$$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ means that there is *at least one* element in the universal set $U$ that gives a true statement when substituted into the predicate $P.$\n\nFor example, $$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n$$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ **such that** $2 \\not\\mid n.$\n\nThis can be further rephrased using plain English as simply:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There is an integer that is odd.*\n\nSimilar to the universal quantifier, we can simply write $\\exists x, \\, P(x)$ if $U$ is clear from the context." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a890270-27c1-52c0-a987-6948933acb97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8308 :tutorial/title "The Existential Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "daf23f50-7d2e-5502-8fae-1f6557f34364" :step/math-academy-id 145581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151813 :question/id #uuid "60f06f02-4fa0-5725-8a87-64497a292a2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12742"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401783}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "8535f2c2-e521-5cfb-8d8c-b5d82a4ebcc6" :step/math-academy-id 145582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153448 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's consider the formal definition of the existential quantifier.\n\nThe **existential quantification** of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as $$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x).$$ This statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for at least one element $x\\in U,$ i.e., if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate is not empty: $$T_P \\neq \\emptyset$$\n\nFor example, consider the quantified statement\n\n$$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n.$$\n\nNotice that the universal set is $U = \\mathbb{Z}$ and the truth set of the underlying predicate\n\n$$P(n): \\:\\: 2 \\not\\mid n$$\n\nis $T_P = \\{\\pm 1,\\pm 3, \\pm 5, \\ldots \\},$ the set of odd numbers. Since $T_P$ is not empty, the statement $\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n$ is true." :tutorial/id #uuid "aa981bd2-681c-57a1-b6a0-c3b9110e0021" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8310 :tutorial/title "The Formal Definition of the Existential Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "67c09639-5532-5323-9fdc-8266af5180c4" :step/math-academy-id 145585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151811 :question/id #uuid "48c68085-0e37-5a70-b927-5046bc62a756" :question/math-academy-id "e-12736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381866}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "a8d4d64a-55cb-58ec-9d64-db4c619082d1" :step/math-academy-id 145586}] :activity/title "Universal and Existential Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152377}]}
